\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we first demonstrated that the global convergence of the classical inexact Newton method, without any particular modifications, can be enhanced by leveraging the structure of the inner solver and considering broader function classes. We then introduced the \emph{Faithful-Newton} (FN) framework---a new family of Newton-type methods---and instantiated it using conjugate residual as the inner solver combined with a backtracking line-search strategy, resulting in the method \texttt{FNCR-LS}. Under the class of functions with Lipschitz-continuous gradients, we showed that \texttt{FNCR-LS} matches the convergence rate of gradient descent. For functions with Lipschitz-continuous Hessians, we established a linear convergence rate in terms of iterations, which can be made independent of the condition number. We also made explicit the trade-off between the time spent performing iterations to solve the subproblem and its implications on both the iteration complexity and the overall operational cost.

We further extended the framework to handle general convex objectives through a regularized variant, \texttt{FNCR-reg-LS}, and proved an iteration complexity that matches the best known rates among non-accelerated methods. Finally, we validated our theoretical results through several empirical studies, demonstrating the practical performance of \texttt{FNCR-LS} and \texttt{FNCR-reg-LS} in comparison with several other second-order methods.

