Table \ref{tab:f1_macro_results} presents the F1-Macro scores  of various stance detection methods across a wide range of datasets.
Encoder-based and decoder-based large language models (LLMs) are the two groups into which methods are separated according to the architectural differences in their construction.   With F1-Macro scores ranging from 36.50\% to 86.58\%, encoder-based models, such as BERT, RoBERTa, XLM-R, and their multilingual or fine-tuned variations, are commonly employed and generally perform well on the majority of datasets.   Interestingly, the CLiCS dataset scored the highest across all encoder-based methods that combined sentiment features with BERTweet.   However, decoder-based LLMs like GPT-3.5, GPT-4, Zephyr, and Mistral perform competitively on a variety of datasets; on ClimateMiSt, GPT-4-based models had an 89.53\% success rate, indicating the growing power of generative models in stance detection tasks.

For datasets involving multiple languages sucha as R-ITA, E-FRA, the reported F1-Macro scores represent the average performance across the languages. The $_{ft}$ subscript indicates that the corresponding language model has been fine-tuned on the specific dataset. These results highlight the adaptability of both encoder- and decoder-based models to various domains and languages within stance detection tasks.

