\subsection{Decision problems}\strut
\indent As with all positional games, we study the complexity of achievement positional games depending on the size of the edges. For this, we must define an adequate decision problem. Given an achievement positional game in which all blue edges have size at most $p$ and all red edges have size at most $q$, the basic question should be of the following form:\\

\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{cXc}
    \hphantom{mmm} &
    Does $\,\,\left\{\begin{array}{l} \text{Left} \\ \text{Right}
    \end{array}\right.\,\,$ have a $\,\,\left\{\begin{array}{l} \text{winning} \\ \text{non-losing}
    \end{array}\right.\,\,$ strategy as $\,\,\left\{\begin{array}{l} \text{first} \\ \text{second}
    \end{array}\right.\,\,$ player?
    & \hphantom{mmm}
\end{tabularx}\\

It actually suffices to address one of these combinations. First of all, we need only consider strategies for Left, as the question for Right is the same up to swapping $E_L$ and $p$ with $E_R$ and $q$ respectively. Moreover, the problem of non-losing strategies is the complement of the problem of winning strategies. Finally, we can assume that Left starts, as this problem admits a linear-time reduction to the case where Right starts. Indeed, Left has a winning strategy on $\Ga=(V,E_L,E_R)$ as second player if and only if, for all $u \in V$, we have $\{u\} \not\in E_R$ and Left has a winning strategy on $\Ga^u$ as first player (note that $\Ga^u$ still has the property that all blue edges have size at most $p$ and all red edges have size at most $q$). All in all, we introduce the following decision problem. \\

\begin{tabularx}{0.95\textwidth}{|l @{} l @{} X|}
	\hline
	\multicolumn{3}{|l|}{\,\,\prob{$p$}{$q$}} \\ \hline
	Input $\,$ & : \,\, & An achievement positional game $\Ga=(V,E_L,E_R)$ such that every element of $E_L$ has size at most $p$ and every element of $E_R$ has size at most $q$. \\
	Output $\,$ & : \,\, & {\sf T} if Left has a winning strategy on $\Ga$ as first player, {\sf F} otherwise. \\ \hline
\end{tabularx} \\

We can start by noticing that, for all $p,q$, \prob{$p$}{$q$} is in {\sf PSPACE}. This is due to the fact that the game cannot last more than $|V|$ moves, so even a brute-force algorithm only needs a space $O(|V|)$ to determine the winner (see \cite[Lemma 2.2]{schaefer} for more details). 

This problem encompasses the decision problems associated to the Maker-Maker and Maker-Breaker conventions. \\

\begin{tabularx}{0.95\textwidth}{|l @{} l @{} X|}
	\hline
	\multicolumn{3}{|l|}{\,\,\makermaker{$k$}} \\ \hline
	Input $\,$ & : \,\, & A hypergraph $H$ in which every edge has size at most $k$. \\
	Output $\,$ & : \,\, & {\sf T} if the first player has a winning strategy for the Maker-Maker game on $H$, {\sf F} otherwise. \\ \hline
\end{tabularx} \\

\begin{tabularx}{0.95\textwidth}{|l @{} l @{} X|}
	\hline
	\multicolumn{3}{|l|}{\,\,\makerbreaker{$k$}} \\ \hline
	Input $\,$ & : \,\, & A hypergraph $H$ in which every edge has size at most $k$. \\
	Output $\,$ & : \,\, & {\sf T} if Maker has a winning strategy as first player for the Maker-Breaker game on $H$, {\sf F} otherwise. \\ \hline
\end{tabularx} \\

Note that \makermaker{$k$} is the subproblem of \prob{$k$}{$k$} where $E_L=E_R$. Moreover, \makerbreaker{$k$} is exactly equivalent to \prob{$k$}{$0$}. Indeed, in \prob{$k$}{$0$}, we have $E_R=\varnothing$ and Right is therefore reduced to a ``Breaker'' role, the only difference being that a Breaker win is considered a draw. Similarly, \prob{$k$}{$1$} is also equivalent to \makerbreaker{$k$}. Indeed, the case where $E_R$ has at least two elements of size 1 is trivial, and the case where $E_R$ has exactly one element of size 1 reduces to \prob{$k$}{$0$} after one round of optimal play.

Let us recall known complexity results for the Maker-Maker and Maker-Breaker conventions. It is easy to see that \makermaker{$2$} and \makerbreaker{$2$} are in {\sf LSPACE} \cite{RW20}. Moreover, \makerbreaker{$3$} is in {\sf P} \cite{MBrank3}. As for hardness results, it has recently been shown that \makerbreaker{$5$} is {\sf PSPACE}-complete \cite{MBrank5}, improving on the previously known result for \makerbreaker{$6$} \cite{MBrank6}. Using a simple argument by Byskov \cite{byskov}, this implies that \makermaker{$6$} is {\sf PSPACE}-complete. The algorithmic complexity of \makermaker{$3$}, \makermaker{$4$}, \makermaker{$5$} and \makerbreaker{$4$} is currently open.

We immediately get the following complexity results for achievement positional games.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:complexity}
    \prob{$k$}{$0$} and \prob{$k$}{$1$} are in {\sf LSPACE} for $k \geq 2$, in {\sf P} for $k = 3$, but are {\sf PSPACE}-complete for $k \geq 5$. Moreover, \prob{$0$}{$k$} and \prob{$1$}{$k$} are in {\sf LSPACE} for all $k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
     It is clear that \prob{$0$}{$k$} and \prob{$1$}{$k$} are in {\sf LSPACE} for all $k$: indeed, if all the red edges have size 1, then Left has a winning strategy as first player if and only if $E_R \neq \varnothing$. The results on \prob{$k$}{$0$} and \prob{$k$}{$1$} are a consequence of the known results (listed above) about \makerbreaker{$k$} and the fact that this problem is equivalent to \prob{$k$}{$0$} and \prob{$k$}{$1$}.
\end{proof}

