\subsection{\prob{3}{3} is {\sf PSPACE}-complete}\strut
\indent The classic \QBF~decision problem can be formulated in terms of the following game. The input is a logic formula $\phi$ in CNF form, with clauses of size exactly 3 and an even number of variables which have a prescribed ordering $x_1,\ldots,x_{2n}$. Two players, Satisfier and Falsifier, take turns choosing valuations for the variables. Satisfier goes first, setting $x_1$ to \true~or \false. Falsifier then sets $x_2$ to \true~or \false, and so on until a full valuation $\mu$ is built. Note that Satisfier (resp. Falsifier) takes care of all variables whose index is odd (resp. even). Satisfier wins the game if $\mu$ satisfies $\phi$, otherwise Falsifier wins. \\

\begin{tabularx}{0.95\textwidth}{|l @{} l @{} X|}
	\hline
	\multicolumn{3}{|l|}{\,\,\QBF} \\ \hline
	Input $\,$ & : \,\, & A logic formula $\phi$ in CNF form, with clauses of size exactly 3, and an even number of variables with a prescribed ordering $x_1,\ldots,x_{2n}$. \\
	Output $\,$ & : \,\, & {\sf T} if Satisfier has a winning strategy, {\sf F} otherwise. \\ \hline
\end{tabularx} \\

Stockmeyer and Meyer~\cite{QBF} have shown that \QBF~is {\sf PSPACE}-complete. We now prove the {\sf PSPACE}-hardness of \prob{$3$}{$3$} using a reduction from \QBF.

\begin{theorem}\label{theo:33}
    \prob{$3$}{$3$} is {\sf PSPACE}-complete.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    Let $\phi$ be a logic formula in CNF form, with clauses of size exactly 3 and variables $x_1,\ldots,x_{2n}$. We are going to build an achievement positional game $\Ga$ with edges of size at most 3 such that Left has a winning strategy on $\Ga$ as second player if and only if Falsifier has a winning strategy on $\phi$. Before giving the formal definition of $\Ga$, let us explain the idea. We see Left as Falsifier and Right as Satisfier. For each variable $x_i$, we will create (among others) four vertices $t_{i,R}$, $f_{i,R}$, $t_{i,L}$, $f_{i,L}$. There will be two phases in $\Ga$.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Phase 1: Both players take turns choosing valuations for the variables $x_i$, in increasing order of $i$. More precisely:
        \begin{itemize}[nolistsep,noitemsep]
            \item[--] For all odd $i$, Right will be forced at some point to pick either $t_{i,R}$ or $f_{i,R}$, which corresponds to her setting $x_i$ to \true~or \false~respectively.
            \item[--] For all even $i$, Left will be forced at some point to pick either $t_{i,L}$ or $f_{i,L}$, which corresponds to him setting $x_i$ to \true~or \false~respectively.
            % \item[--] For all odd $i$, Right will be forced to pick either $t_{i,R}$ (which corresponds to her setting $x_i$ to \true, and forces Left to pick $f_{i,L}$ in response) or $f_{i,R}$ (which corresponds to her setting $x_i$ to \false, and forces Left to pick $t_{i,L}$ in response).
            % \item[--] For all even $i$, Left will be forced to pick either $t_{i,L}$ (which corresponds to him setting $x_i$ to \true, and forces Right to pick $f_{i,R}$ in response) or $f_{i,L}$ (which corresponds to him setting $x_i$ to \false, and forces Right to pick $t_{i,R}$ in response).
        \end{itemize}
        Additionally to the edges that will be there to force both players' moves, there will be blue \textit{clause-edges} corresponding to the clauses of $\phi$. The important thing is that the clause-edges will only contain $t_{i,R}$'s and $f_{i,R}$'s, so that Left never plays inside them during Phase 1: this ensures that he does not create threats with the clause-edges, so that his moves are indeed forced. If the valuation $\mu$ built by both players satisfies $\phi$, then Right will have picked at least one vertex in every clause-edge by the end of Phase 1, thus ensuring that she will draw the game.
        \item Phase 2: Left picks the complementary vertices to those picked by Right during Phase 1. This means that, for all $i$, Left picks $t_{i,R}$ (resp. $f_{i,R}$) if Right had picked $f_{i,R}$ (resp. $t_{i,R}$). This way, if $\mu$ does not satisfy $\phi$, then Left will fill a clause-edge during Phase 2, thus winning the game.
    \end{itemize}
    Let us now define $\Ga=(V,E_L,E_R)$ rigorously. %The vertex set $V$ contains vertices $t_{i,R}$, $f_{i,R}$, $t_{i,L}$, $f_{i,L}$, $u_i$, $v_i$, $a_i$, $b_i$, $c_i$ for all $1 \leq i \leq 2n$, plus an additional vertex $w$. The edges sets $E_R$ and $E_L$ are defined as follows.

    \begin{itemize}
        \item We define $V= \bigcup_{1 \leq i \leq 2n} (V_i \cup \{a_i,b_i,b'_i,c_i,c'_i\}) \cup \{w\}$ where:
            \begin{itemize}
                \item[--] for all $1 \leq i \leq 2n$: $V_i=\{t_{i,R},f_{i,R},t_{i,L},f_{i,L},u_i,v_i\}$;
                \item[--] for all $1 \leq i \leq 2n$: the vertices $a_i$, $b_i$, $b'_i$, $c_i$ and $c'_i$ are only relevant during Phase 2;
                \item[--] $w$ is an additional vertex.
            \end{itemize}
        \item We define $E_R = \bigcup_{1 \leq i \leq 2n} E_R^{(i)}$ and $E_L = \bigcup_{1 \leq i \leq 2n} E_L^{(i)} \cup \{e_w\} \cup \widetilde{E_L} \cup E_L^{\,\phi}$ where:
            \begin{itemize}
                \item[--] The edges in $E_R^{(i)},E_L^{(i)}$ are here to force both    player's moves during Phase 1. For convenience, we introduce the following notation. When the notation $e^*$ appears inside the definition of $E_R^{(i)}$, it means we add the edge $e$ if $i=1$, or the two edges $e \cup \{t_{i-1,R}\}$ and $e \cup \{f_{i-1,R}\}$ if $i \geq 2$. The point is that Right will pick one of $t_{i-1,R}$ or $f_{i-1,R}$, at which point the updated red edge will be $e$. Similarly, when the notation $e^*$ appears inside the definition of $E_L^{(i)}$, it means we add the edge $e$ if $i=1$, or the two edges $e \cup \{t_{i-1,L}\}$ and $e \cup \{f_{i-1,L}\}$ if $i \geq 2$.
                \\ For odd $i$, we define $E_R^{(i)}=\{\{t_{i,R},f_{i,L}\}^*,\{f_{i,R},t_{i,L}\}^*,\{f_{i,R},f_{i,L},u_i\},\{t_{i,R},t_{i,L},v_i\}\}$ and $E_L^{(i)}=\{\{t_{i,R},f_{i,R}\}^*,\{t_{i,R},u_i\}^*,\{f_{i,R},v_i\}^*,\{t_{i,L},u_i\}^*,\{f_{i,L},v_i\}^*\}$.
                \\ For even $i$, we define $E_L^{(i)}=\{\{t_{i,R},f_{i,L}\}^*,\{f_{i,R},t_{i,L}\}^*,\{t_{i,L},f_{i,L},u_i\},\{t_{i,L},f_{i,L},v_i\}\}$ and $E_R^{(i)}=\{\{t_{i,L},f_{i,L}\}^*,\{t_{i,L},u_i\}^*,\{f_{i,L},v_i\}^*,\{t_{i,R},u_i\}^*,\{f_{i,R},v_i\}^*,\{t_{i,R},t_{i,L},v_i\},\{f_{i,R},f_{i,L},u_i\}\}$.
                \item[--] The blue edge $e_w=\{u_{2n},v_{2n},w\}$ is here to force Right to waste a move as we transition from Phase 1 to Phase 2.
                \item[--] The blue edges in $\widetilde{E_L}=\bigcup_{1 \leq i \leq 2n}\{\{t_{i,R},a_i,b_i\},\{t_{i,R},a_i,c_i\},\{f_{i,R},a_i,b'_i\},\{f_{i,R},a_i,c'_i\}\}$ form butterflies which are here to force Right's moves during Phase 2.
                \item[--] The blue clause-edges in $E_L^{\,\phi}$ are defined as follows. To every literal $\ell_i \in \{x_i,\overline{x_i}\}$, we associate a vertex $v(\ell_i) \in V$: if $i$ is odd then $v(x_i)=t_{i,R}$ and $v(\overline{x_i})=f_{i,R}$; if $i$ is even then $v(x_i)=f_{i,R}$ and $v(\overline{x_i})=t_{i,R}$. For each clause $\ell_{i_1} \vee \ell_{i_2} \vee \ell_{i_3}$ in $\phi$, we add the clause-edge $\{v(\ell_{i_1}),v(\ell_{i_2}),v(\ell_{i_3})\}$.
            \end{itemize}
    \end{itemize}
    For visual aid, the game obtained for $n=2$, without the clause edges, is shown in Figure \ref{fig:Gadget_3_3}. Now, let us study how the game unfolds.

    \begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*)]

        \item An important remark is that $\{t_{1,R},f_{1,L}\}$ and $\{f_{1,R},t_{1,L}\}$ are the only red edges of size 2 at the beginning of the game. We claim that Right is forced to pick $t_{1,R}$ or $f_{1,R}$ as her first move. Indeed, if she picks $t_{1,L}$, then Left picks $f_{1,R}$, and Right cannot defend the double threat of the blue edges $\{t_{1,R},f_{1,R}\}$ and $\{f_{1,R},v_1\}$. Similarly, she cannot pick $f_{1,L}$. If she picks some vertex in $V \setminus \{t_{1,R},f_{1,R},t_{1,L},f_{1,L}\}$, then Left's answer is not forced, so he can pick $f_{1,R}$ to win with the same double threat as before (unless Right has picked $v_1$ specifically, in which case he picks $t_{1,R}$ instead and also has a double threat with $u_1$). This shows that Right is forced to pick $t_{1,R}$ or $f_{1,R}$ as her first move. By symmetry, assume Right has picked $t_{1,R}$ (Figure \ref{fig:GadgetOdd} with $i=1$ actually illustrates both cases). A sequence of four forced moves is now triggered:
        \begin{itemize}[nolistsep,noitemsep]
            \item[--] Left is forced to pick $f_{1,L}$ because of the red edge $\{t_{1,R},f_{1,L}\}$.
            \item[--] Right is forced to pick $v_1$ because of the blue edge $\{f_{1,L},v_1\}$.
            \item[--] Left is forced to pick $t_{1,L}$ because of the red edge $\{t_{1,R},t_{1,L},v_1\}$.
            \item[--] Right is forced to pick $u_1$ because of the blue edge $\{t_{1,L},u_1\}$.
        \end{itemize}

        \begin{figure}[h]
	       \centering
	       \includegraphics[scale=.55]{GadgetOdd2.eps}
	       \caption{For odd $i$, the updated edges induced by $V_i$ after the first $5(i-1)$ moves. The red (resp. blue) vertices are those that are thereafter picked by Right (resp. Left), when Right chooses to pick $t_{i,R}$ (left) or $f_{i,R}$ (right). For clarity, edges of size 2 are represented by simple lines.}\label{fig:GadgetOdd}
        \end{figure}

        After these first five moves, all red edges and blue edges induced by $V_1$ are dead. Moreover, each edge of the form $e^*$ in the definitions of $E_R^{(2)}$ and $E_L^{(2)}$ has now yielded the updated edge $e$ since Right has picked one of $t_{1,R}$ or $f_{1,R}$ and Left has picked one of $t_{1,L}$ or $f_{1,L}$ (in fact, he has picked both).

        \item Therefore, $\{t_{2,R},f_{2,L}\}$ and $\{f_{2,R},t_{2,L}\}$ are the only updated blue edges of size 2 at this point in the game. We claim that Left is forced to pick $t_{2,L}$ or $f_{2,L}$ as his next move. Indeed, if he picks $t_{2,R}$, then Right picks $f_{2,L}$, and Left cannot defend the double threat of the red edges $\{t_{2,L},f_{2,L}\}$ and $\{f_{2,L},v_2\}$. Similarly, he cannot pick $f_{2,R}$. If he picks some vertex in $V \setminus \{t_{2,R},f_{2,R},t_{2,L},f_{2,L}\}$, then Right's answer is not forced, so she can pick $f_{2,L}$ to win with the same double threat as before (unless Left has picked $v_2$ specifically, in which case she picks $t_{2,L}$ instead and also has a double threat with $u_2$). This shows that Left is forced to pick $t_{2,L}$ or $f_{2,L}$ as his next move. By symmetry, assume Left has picked $t_{2,L}$ (Figure \ref{fig:GadgetEven} with $i=2$ actually illustrates both cases). A sequence of four forced moves is now triggered:
        \begin{itemize}[nolistsep,noitemsep]
            \item[--] Right is forced to pick $f_{2,R}$ because of the blue edge $\{t_{2,L},f_{2,R}\}$.
            \item[--] Left is forced to pick $v_2$ because of the red edge $\{f_{2,R},v_2\}$.
            \item[--] Right is forced to pick $f_{2,L}$ because of the blue edge $\{t_{2,L},f_{2,L},v_2\}$.
            \item[--] Left is forced to pick $u_2$ because of the red edge $\{f_{2,R},f_{2,L},u_2\}$.
        \end{itemize}

        \begin{figure}[h]
	       \centering
	       \includegraphics[scale=.55]{GadgetEven2.eps}
