        After these five moves, all red edges and blue edges induced by $V_2$ are dead. Moreover, each edge of the form $e^*$ in the definitions of $E_R^{(3)}$ and $E_L^{(3)}$ has now yielded the updated edge $e$ since Right has picked one of $t_{2,R}$ or $f_{2,R}$ and Left has picked one of $t_{2,L}$ or $f_{2,L}$.

        \item Therefore, we now have the same situation for $i=3$ as we had for $i=1$ at the beginning of the game, with Right being forced to pick either $t_{3,R}$ or $f_{3,R}$ and triggering a forced sequence of the next four moves (as in Figure \ref{fig:GadgetOdd}). The game continues like this, with moves number $5i-4$ to $5i$ obeying the same rules as moves 1 to 5 for odd $i$ or as moves 6 to 10 for even $i$. Phase 1 thus ends without a winner.
        %with Right and Left having taken turns choosing valuations for the $x_i$'s (Right for odd $i$, Left for even $i$).
        All the red edges in the $E_R^{(i)}$'s and all the blue edges in the $E_L^{(i)}$'s are dead at this point. In particular, Right cannot have a winning strategy in $\Ga$, and her only goal from here on is to prevent Left from filling a blue edge.

        \item The last two (forced) moves of Left consisted in picking $u_{2n}$ and $v_{2n}$, so Right is forced to pick $w$ because of the blue edge $e_w$. At this point, the unpicked vertices are: the $a_i$'s, $b_i$'s and $c_i$'s, as well as half of the $t_{i,R}$'s and $f_{i,R}$'s (the other half having been picked by Right). Left is next to play.

        \item Phase 2 now begins. Let $\mu$ be the valuation defined as follows. For all odd $i$, $\mu(x_i)=\true$~if and only if Right has picked $t_{i,R}$. For all even $i$, $\mu(x_i)=\true$~if and only if Left has picked $t_{i,L}$ \textit{i.e.} if and only if Right has picked $f_{i,R}$. By definition of $\mu$, we have that, for any literal $\ell$, $\mu(\ell)=\true$~if and only if Right has picked the vertex $v(\ell)$. Therefore, $\mu$ satisfies $\phi$ if and only if all the clause-edges are dead by this point.

        \begin{itemize}
            \item Firstly, suppose Satisfier has a winning strategy $\strat$ on $\phi$. We show that Right has a non-losing strategy on $\Ga$. We assume that, for all odd $i$ during Phase 1, Right has chosen between $t_{i,R}$ or $f_{i,R}$ according to $\strat$, so that $\mu$ satisfies $\phi$. This ensures that all the clause-edges are dead. Therefore, all Right now has to do is take care of the blue edges in $\widetilde{E_L}$, half of which are dead already (each blue butterfly only has one ``wing'' left). This is easily done via a pairing strategy, consisting in pairing together the vertices $t_{i,R}$ and $a_i$ (resp. $f_{i,R}$ and $a_i$) if Right had picked $f_{i,R}$ (resp. $t_{i,R}$) during Phase 1.
            \item Lastly, suppose Falsifier has a winning strategy $\strat$ on $\phi$. We show that Left has a winning strategy on $\Ga$. We assume that, for all even $i$ during Phase 1, Left has chosen between $t_{i,L}$ or $f_{i,L}$ according to $\strat$, so that $\mu$ does not satisfy $\phi$. This ensures that there exists a clause-edge $e$ that is not dead \textit{i.e.} whose three vertices are all unpicked. To ensure that he fills $e$, Left simply goes on to pick all the $t_{i,R}$'s and $f_{i,R}$'s that have not been picked yet. The reason why Right cannot prevent this is because, whenever Left picks some $t_{i,R}$ (resp. $f_{i,R}$), Right is forced to answer by picking one of $a_i$, $b_i$, $b'_i$, $c_i$ or $c'_i$, otherwise Left would pick $a_i$ next and win because of the double threat of the blue edges $\{t_{i,R},a_i,b_i\}$ and $\{t_{i,R},a_i,c_i\}$ (resp. $\{f_{i,R},a_i,b'_i\}$ and $\{f_{i,R},a_i,c'_i\}$). \qedhere
        \end{itemize}
    \end{enumerate}
