they are the only proof rules in $\mathcal{B}$ with conclusion $p$ or $q$, such atomic proofs have to end either with an application of $R1 - p$ or of $R1 - q$. Assume that the final rule is an application of $R1 - p$. Then either the right premise with shape $p$ of this final application $R1 - p$ is a proof assumption, in which case the deduction cannot be an atomic proof, or it is a deduction showing $q ; \emptyset \vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{B}} p$ (which might already be a proof of $p$, as $q$ does not need to effectively occur in the deduction showing this). If it is a deduction showing $q ; \emptyset \vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{B}} p$ then this deduction must also end with an application of $R1 - p$, which once again requires a deduction showing $q ; \emptyset \vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{B}} p$ for its right premise. The same reasoning applies if instead we consider the penultimate rule of the refutation to be an application of $R1-q$. As such, for any natural number $n$ we can show that there is no deduction in $\mathcal{B}$ with $n$ rule applications that is an atomic refutation of $r$ (since after reaching a deduction with $n$ rule applications we would require yet another deduction for one of the premises), hence since atomic proofs are always finite conclude that there is no refutation of $r$ in $\mathcal{B}$. This allows us to conclude $\nvdash^{-}_{\mathcal{B}} r$ and thus $\nVdash^{-}_{\mathcal{B}} r$ by \ref{c:at-}, as desired.

