\begin{proof}
     Assume  $\Gamma_{\At} ; \Delta_{\At} \vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{B}} p$, and let $\mathcal{C}$ be any extension of $\mathcal{B}$ with $\Vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} q$ for all $q \in \Gamma_{\At}$ and $\Vdash^{-}_{\mathcal{C}} r$ for all $r \in \Delta_{\At}$. Then, by \ref{c:at+} and \ref{c:at-},  $\vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} q$ for all $q \in \Gamma_{\At}$ and $\vdash^{-}_{\mathcal{C}} r$ for all $r \in \Delta_{\At}$. Since $\mathcal{C}$ is an extension of $\mathcal{B}$ all deductions in $\mathcal{B}$ are deductions in $\mathcal{C}$, we conclude $\Gamma_{\At} ; \Delta_{\At} \vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} p$. Hence by composing all deductions we get a proof of $\vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} p$ and thus conclude $\Vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} p$. By arbitrariness of $\mathcal{C}$ and \ref{c:inf+} we conclude  $\Gamma_{\At} ; \Delta_{\At} \Vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{B}} p$.

    \noindent For the converse, assume $\Gamma_{\At} ; \Delta_{\At} \Vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{B}} p$. Let $\mathcal{C}$ be the base obtained by adding to $\mathcal{B}$ one proof rule concluding $q$ from empty premises for every $q \in \Gamma_{\At}$ and one refutation rule concluding $r$ from empty premises for every $r \in \Delta_{\At}$. Then a single application of each of those rules yields $\vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} q$ for all $q \in \Gamma_{\At}$ and $\vdash^{-}_{\mathcal{C}} r$ for all $r \in \Delta_{\At}$, so, by \ref{c:at+} and \ref{c:at-}, also $\Vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} q$ for all $q \in \Gamma_{\At}$ and $\Vdash^{-}_{\mathcal{C}} r$ for all $r \in \Delta_{\At}$. This, together with our original assumption and \ref{c:inf+}, yields $\Vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} p$ and thus $\vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} p$. If $\Pi$ is a proof of $\vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{C}} p$ in $\mathcal{C}$, let $\Pi'$ be the deduction obtained by substituting each of the proof rules added to $\mathcal{B}$ to obtain $\mathcal{C}$ by a proof assumption with the same shape, as well as each of the refutation rules added to $\mathcal{B}$ to obtain $\mathcal{C}$ by a refutation assumption with the same shape (e.g. a proof rule concluding $q$ from no premises is replaced by a proof assumption with shape $p$, and a refutation rule concluding $r$ from no premises is replaced by a refutation assumption with shape $r$). Since all the new rules of $\mathcal{C}$ are removed by this procedure the remaining rules all belong to $\mathcal{B}$, so $\Pi'$ is a deduction showing $\Theta_{\At}; \Sigma_{\At} \vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{B}} p$ for $\Theta_{\At} \subseteq \Gamma_{\At}$ and $\Sigma_{\At} \subseteq \Delta_{\At}$, hence by the definition of atomic deducibility we conclude $\Gamma_{\At}; \Delta_{\At} \vdash^{+}_{\mathcal{B}} p$. 
\end{proof}



