% Deep learning has revolutionized the world, starting from tasks as simple as image classification and going all the way to language-based autonomous agents, robotics, and life sciences. However, the exponential growth in deep neural network parameters and data volumes has strained traditional computing architectures, primarily due to the data movement bottleneck. To address this challenge, we introduce Machine Intelligence on Wireless Edge Networks (MIWEN) \textemdash\ an approach for deep learning on ultra-low-power edge devices. MIWEN leverages disaggregated memory access to wirelessly stream machine learning models to size, weight, and power-constrained (SWaP) edge devices or clients, mitigating memory and power bottlenecks by integrating computation into the existing radio frequency analog chain of wireless transceivers. MIWEN aims to achieve scalable and efficient implementations, significantly reducing energy consumption and latency compared to conventional digital signal processing systems. 

Deep neural network (DNN) inference on power-constrained edge devices is bottlenecked by costly weight storage and data movement. We introduce MIWEN, a radio frequency (RF) analog \iffalse\zhihui{radio frequency}\fi architecture that “disaggregates” memory by streaming weights wirelessly and performing classification in the analog front end of standard transceivers. By encoding weights and activations onto RF carriers and using native mixers as computation units, MIWEN eliminates local weight memory and the overhead of analog-to-digital and digital-to-analog conversion\iffalse\zhihui{I think we still need some ADC/DAC conversion, but very little?}\fi. We derive the effective number of bits of radio frequency analog computation under thermal noise\iffalse\zhihui{do we have shot noise in RF?}\fi, quantify the energy–precision trade-off, and demonstrate digital-comparable MNIST accuracy at orders-of-magnitude lower energy, unlocking real-time inference on low-power, memory-free, edge devices.


