Machine learning has become a dominant paradigm in modern computing, driving revolutionary advances in computer vision, natural language processing, and autonomous systems~\cite{lecun2015deep, vinyals2019grandmaster, brown2020language}. However, as deep neural networks (DNNs) grow deeper and more complex, they face two critical energy constraints on conventional architectures. First, fundamental thermodynamic limits set a lower bound on the energy required for irreversible bit operations, yet today's machine learning accelerators expend more than six orders of magnitude above this bound~\cite{horowitz20141}. Second, DNN inference relies on iterative \iffalse\zhihui{on GPU, I guess it's parallel but not iterative?}\fi matrix–vector multiplications (MVMs), whose energy and latency are now dominated by moving weight tensors between off-chip memory and processing units, rather than by the computation~\cite{li2017multi, satyanarayanan2017emergence, butt2020review}. These energy and memory–compute bottlenecks severely restrict the deployment of large-scale machine learning models on power-constrained hardware.

Wireless edge networks—composed of numerous devices with stringent power and memory constraints, such as environmental sensors, Internet-of-Things nodes, and autonomous vehicles and drones—are currently incapable of running advanced machine learning models on-device~\cite{satyanarayanan2017emergence}. Although these devices are internet-connected and capable of basic signal processing \cite{sulimany2024quantum}, their limited resources preclude real-time, on-device inference and force frequent offload to remote servers.

In-physics computing has been proposed to reduce the energy cost of machine learning by performing MVMs directly in the analog domain. Integrated photonic DNNs~\cite{shen2017deep, tait2017neuromorphic, ashtiani2022chip, feldmann2019all, feldmann2021parallel, chen2023deep, bandyopadhyay2022single, bernstein2023single, onodera2024scaling, basani2024all,hamerly2019large,sludds2022delocalized}, free-space optical accelerators \cite{lin2018all, zhou2021large, wright2022deep, wang2022optical, wang2023image, ma2023quantum}, memristive crossbars \cite{agarwal2016energy, ambrogio2023analog}, and fiber-based processors \cite{xu202111, davis2022frequency, bacvanski2024qamnet} have been demonstrated to reduce energy power consumption. While these platforms achieve remarkable energy efficiency, they depend on specialized non-CMOS devices and dedicated optical interconnects that are difficult to integrate into existing wireless-edge infrastructures.

In this work, we leverage the native networking capabilities of edge devices to enable memory-free, energy-efficient machine learning. Our architecture, Machine Intelligence on Wireless Edge Networks (MIWEN), disaggregates DNN weight storage by streaming weights wirelessly from a central base station and executing computations in the passive mixer stage of commodity radio transceivers. By encoding both weights and activations onto distinct RF carriers—and by eliminating local weight caches, and digital back-ends—MIWEN can perform DNN inference using off-the-shelf RF electronics. This approach repurposes the cellular RF chain, originally designed for communication, for in-physics computing, and by avoiding on-device model storage, MIWEN also mitigates privacy concerns in machine learning \cite{sulimany2024quantum}.

We analyze a standard RF front-end chain to assess its capability for deep learning tasks. Our protocol involves the broadcast of neural network model weights by a central wireless station. The weights are processed by edge devices using their local weights and the components of their RF circuitry (such as mixers and filters) to produce activations. These activations are then reprocessed with the next set of weights received by the edge device from the broadcasting station (see Fig.~\ref{scheme}). In this way, one can implement multiple layers of a neural network without ever having to store them in the memory of the edge device.

