\subsection{Numerical results}
We wrote custom layers in the popular machine learning package PyTorch to describe the operation of each step of our protocol. This includes all the components of our protocol: the encoding of the activation vectors and the weights into signals $x(t),w(t)$, incorporation of thermal noise at the appropriate points of the physical computation chain, and a physically accurate description of the diode ring mixing process. The custom layers were then strung together into custom neural networks that were trained on the MNIST digit recognition task. 

Fig.~\ref{fig:results} presents the variation of the test accuracy obtained by networks of two different sizes when the total client energy is restricted to various thresholds. Since we are working in the setting of disaggregated memory access where the weights $w(t)$ are streamed to the client by a distant server, we need only be concerned with the energy expended by the client in processing the weights (called `input energy' in Fig.~\ref{fig:results}). The downsampling of MNIST to feed networks of different sizes proceeds via patch-averaging of the original 28-by-28 images. 

The performance of both networks is equivalent to random guessing below the attojoule (aJ) level and rises to near-digital accuracy when the input energy is about {100}\thinspace{picojoules} (pJ). By virtue of the deviation of the diode ring mixer from exact multiplicative mixing at higher energies, the test accuracy experiences a degradation beyond the nanojoule (nJ) level whose severity depends on the network size. The figure presents two kinds of plots for each network size: the dashed lines depict the variation of the test accuracy with total input waveform energy while the solid lines depict the same accuracy, but as a function of the total client energy, which is the sum of the amplification energy cost inside the layernorm layers and the original input waveform energy.  

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
In this work, we proposed Machine Intelligence on Wireless Edge Networks (MIWEN), an RF-communicatons based edge machine learning protocol that endows ultra low-power edge sensors and devices with the ability to execute heavy machine learning workloads without the need for storing the model locally. MIWEN leverages disaggregated memory access to enable the wireless streaming of machine learning models to edge devices, addressing both memory and power bottlenecks. Moreover, we discover that the standard RF analog chain in every cell phone can be utilized to perform the large matrix vector products required in machine learning in an online fashion. This paradigm reduces the dependence on centralized computing resources and paves the way for real-time decision-making in wireless networks.
