\section{Related Work}

\subsection{System-Level Cooperative Perception Architectures}
Cooperative perception systems leverage distributed sensing by vehicles and
infrastructure to extend situational awareness beyond the limitations of
individual agents~\cite{arnold2022cooperative, liu2022when2com, wang2020v2vnet, xu2022opv2v}.
These systems generally fall into decentralized (V2V) or centralized (V2I) paradigms,
with modern deployments adopting hybrid approaches involving edge/cloud fusion~\cite{huang2022multi, arnold2022cooperative}. Real-time synchronization is achieved using
GPS-based clocks or network protocols~\cite{yu2022dair}, but residual asynchrony
and spatial misalignment persist. Some systems (e.g., DAIR-V2X~\cite{yu2022dair},
LUCOOP~\cite{axmann2023lucoop}) explicitly address synchronization by providing both
hardware and software solutions. Bandwidth constraints and communication latency
drive the use of pre-processed features or object lists, rather than raw sensor
data, for cooperative fusion~\cite{chen2019fcooper, arnold2022cooperative}.
Hardware implementations of such systems typically consist LiDAR and camera suites, edge-compute
devices (e.g., NVIDIA Jetson), and standardized frameworks such as ROS. Software
stacks may include V2X middleware (e.g., OpenCDA, Apollo Cyber RT), enabling modular
perception, fusion, and control pipelines.

\subsection{Cooperative Perception Algorithms}
Cooperative perception algorithms can be broadly categorized into early,
intermediate, and late fusion techniques~\cite{wang2020v2vnet, liu2022when2com,
chen2019fcooper}. Early fusion, which merges raw sensor data (e.g., point clouds),
yields the best accuracy but is bandwidth-intensive and requires precise
synchronization when sending to the cloud. Furthermore, late fusion combining high-level detection outputs, is communication-efficient
but may miss occluded objects. 
Lastly, intermediate fusion exchanging neural features
or Bird's Eye View (BEV) grids---offers a balance between accuracy and bandwidth,
with models like V2VNet~\cite{wang2020v2vnet},
V2X-ViT~\cite{xu2022v2xvit}, and DiscoNet~\cite{li2023disconet} are advancing the state
of the art. On the other hand, attention-based communication (When2comm~\cite{liu2022when2com},
Who2comm~\cite{liu2020who2com}) improves efficiency by dynamically selecting information
to transmit. Tracking and motion prediction in cooperative settings remain less
studied, with most works applying multi-object tracking post-fusion~\cite{wang2020v2vnet,
arnold2022cooperative}.

