    The time-complexity results follow from the general techniques we develop in this paper to implement algorithms designed through the graph composition framework~(\cref{thm:circulation-space-reflection-implementation}). These techniques work in the QROM-model (see~\cref{subsec:model}), which relies on weaker assumptions than the QRAM-model. Most of the relevant literature on these problems considers the stronger QRAM-model, so our work constitutes a conceptual improvement over these works~\cite{akmal2023near,wang2024quantum}. See \cref{subsec:model} for a more detailed discussion about the differences between these models.

    For the specific problems considered in this work, i.e., those listed in \cref{tbl:applications}, it is not clear whether the QROM-assumption is necessary. It is conceivable that the time-complexity results also hold in the circuit model. However, it seems unlikely that the general results for time-efficient implementation of algorithms designed through the graph composition framework can be transformed to avoid the QROM-assumption. Since these results aim to illustrate the usage of these more general techniques, we leave the conversion of these time complexity results to the circuit model for future work.

