% The problem of multimodal sarcasm detection has gained increasing attention in recent years due to the proliferation of multimedia posts on social media platforms. To understand sarcastic image-text posts, one often needs to rely on external contextual knowledge, such as cultural references, commonsense expectations, or historical knowledge. While existing models typically operate on the image and text input directly or incorporate shallow external cues such as image captions or object-attribute pairs from the image, they often fall short in capturing the deeper rationale behind sarcasm. 
% To bridge this gap, we propose MiDRE (Mixture of Dual Reasoning Experts), which combines two expert networks: an internal expert that captures incongruities within the image-text pair, and an external expert that leverages structured rationales generated via Chain-of-Thought prompting to a Large Vision-Language Model. To effectively combine these experts, we introduce an adaptive and interpretable gating mechanism that dynamically weighs the contributions of each expert and learns to prioritize the most relevant reasoning path —internal or external—thereby enabling more accurate and robust sarcasm detection. Extensive experiments on two publicly available datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. Various qualitative analyses highlight the crucial role of external rationales, revealing that even when they are occasionally noisy, they provide valuable cues that guide the model toward a better understanding of sarcasm. 

Multimodal sarcasm detection has attracted growing interest due to the rise of multimedia posts on social media. Understanding sarcastic image-text posts often requires external contextual knowledge, such as cultural references or commonsense reasoning. However, existing models struggle to capture the deeper rationale behind sarcasm, relying mainly on shallow cues like image captions or object-attribute pairs from images. To address this, we propose \textbf{MiDRE} (\textbf{Mi}xture of \textbf{D}ual \textbf{R}easoning \textbf{E}xperts), which integrates an internal reasoning expert for detecting incongruities within the image-text pair and an external reasoning expert that utilizes structured rationales generated via Chain-of-Thought prompting to a Large Vision-Language Model. An adaptive gating mechanism dynamically weighs the two experts, selecting the most relevant reasoning path. Experiments on two benchmark datasets show that MiDRE achieves superior performance over baselines. Various qualitative analyses highlight the crucial role of external rationales, revealing that even when they are occasionally noisy, they provide valuable cues that guide the model toward a better understanding of sarcasm. 

\end{abstract}

