\subsection{Rationale Generation Module}
This module, illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:rationale_module}, is responsible for generating external reasoning or rationales about why a given image-text pair is sarcastic or not, by leveraging the pretrained knowledge of LVLMs. Sarcasm can emerge from either one modality alone or through an interplay between both modalities. To capture these diverse scenarios, we extend the idea of CoT prompting to the multimodal setting by sequentially guiding LVLM to reason in three independent stages: image-only, text-only, and joint image-text. 

We observe that when the LVLM was prompted with both the image and text to generate all three rationales together, it often blended the sources—such as incorporating image details into the text-only rationale, and vice versa. To avoid this cross-modal interference and ensure that each rationale reflects its intended modality, we designed an independent three-stage extraction process. This setup helps maintain clear boundaries between image, text, and combined reasoning.

The three-stage process is described below.

\textbf{Stage 1- Image-based rationale generation:} In this stage, the LVLM is prompted to focus exclusively on the image, instructing it to describe the image content, infer possible implications, and reason whether the visual content alone could indicate sarcasm or irony. This helps capture cues like meme-style content or ironic visual contexts. This stage can be formulated as:
$R^{I} = LVLM(Prompt^{I}, I)$

\textbf{Stage 2- Text-based rationale generation:} Here, the LVLM is prompted to consider only the text to identify signs of sarcasm. This stage isolates the textual sarcasm signal without visual influence. We formulate this stage as:
$R^{T} = LVLM(Prompt^{T}, T)$

\textbf{Stage 3- Multimodal rationale generation:} In the final stage, both image and text are provided as input, and the LVLM is prompted to reason about the relationship between them. The goal of this stage is to identify cross-modal inconsistencies. We can formulate this stage as follows:
$R^{M} = LVLM(Prompt^{M}, I, T)$

We combine the three rationales to form a comprehensive external reasoning input $R = [R^{I}, R^{T}, R^{M}]$. This combined rationale
encapsulates detailed external reasoning signals from each modality and their interaction. \textit{The order of combination of rationales is arbitrary as we observed very little variation in performance during our experiments}.

\subsection{Dual Reasoning Expert Networks}
The internal reasoning expert $(IR)$, external reasoning expert $(ER)$ and the adaptive gate are deeply integrated into every encoder layers of the LLM. Once the rationale $R$ is extracted from the rationale generation module, the modified input sample becomes $x = \{I, T, \hat{T}\}$, where $\hat{T} = [T, R]$ is the combination of text and rationale. $ER$ considers the image-text along with rationales as input. The input required for $ER$ is extracted as 
\begin{equation}
H_{I} = VE(I), \quad H_{\hat{T}}^{0} = E_{LLM}(\hat{T})
\end{equation}
\noindent where $H_{I} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times d_{v}}$ is the image embedding from CLIP visual encoder $VE(\cdot)$, and $m$ is the number of image patches. $H_{\hat{T}}^{0} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d_{t}}$ is the embedding of the combined text and rationale input $\hat{T}$ from LLM embedding layer $E_{LLM}(\cdot)$, where $n$ is the token length.
Since image embeddings $H_{I}$ is from the CLIP image encoder, to align it with the LLM embedding space, we use a separate learnable projection layer:
\begin{equation}
H_{I^{\prime}} = H_{I}W_{i}  
\end{equation}
$\hat{T}$ contains richer and more complex semantic information than $T$, hence we pass it through the transformer layers of the encoder to leverage the deep contextual reasoning capabilities of the LLM and facilitate more sophisticated integration of external rationale signals.

Specifically in LLM encoder layer ${i}$, the computation of $ER$ can be formulated as:
\begin{equation}
 M_{ER}^{i} = ER^{i}(H_{\hat{T}}^{i-1}, H_{I^{\prime}}) 
\end{equation}
\noindent where  $M_{ER}^{i}$ is the output from the external reasoning expert $ER^{i}$ in the encoder layer $i$. $ER$ is implemented like a cross-attention mechanism as follows:
\begin{equation}
  ER(H_{\hat{T}}, H_{I^{\prime}}) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{\hat{T}}K^{T}_{I^{\prime}}}{\sqrt{d}}\right) V_{I^{\prime}}  
\end{equation}
\noindent where $\{Q_{\hat{T}}, K^{T}_{I^{\prime}}, V_{I^{\prime}}\} = \{H_{\hat{T}}W_{Q}, H_{I^{\prime}}W_{K}, H_{I^{\prime}}W_{V}\}$. $\{W_{Q}, W_{K}, W_{V}\} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d_{k}}$ are query, key and value projections respectively.

The internal reasoning expert ($IR$) is designed to learn semantic associations directly from the input image-text pair without relying on any external rationales. Since $T$ is comparatively simpler than the enriched rationale-augmented input $\hat{T}$, and to avoid potential interference or leakage of rationale information into the internal reasoning stream, we extract the embedding of $T$ separately using a CLIP text encoder. This design choice ensures that $IR$ operates purely on the original image and text content.
\textit{To validate this design choice, we also experimented with a reversed configuration—feeding $T$ into the LLM encoder and $\hat{T}$ into the CLIP text encoder, but this led to inferior performance, as reported in Appendix A.2.}

The text input required for $IR$ is extracted as $H_{T} = VE(T)$

where $H_{T} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d_{t}}$ is the embedding of text only input $T$ from CLIP text encoder $TE(\cdot)$. \noindent Since text embeddings $H_{T}$ is from CLIP encoder, to align it with the LLM embedding space, we use a separate learnable projection layer:
\begin{equation}
H_{T^{\prime}} = H_{T}W_{t}  
\end{equation}
We utilize the same image embeddings ${H}_{I^{\prime}}$ as of $ER$. At each LLM encoder layer $i$, it produces the output:
\begin{equation}
 M_{IR}^{i} = IR^{i}(H_{T^{\prime}}, H_{I^{\prime}})
\end{equation}
The internal reasoning expert is realized as:
\begin{equation}
  IR(H_{T^{\prime}}, H_{I^{\prime}}) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{T^{\prime}}K^{T}_{I^{\prime}}}{\sqrt{d}}\right) V_{I^{\prime}}
\end{equation}
\noindent where $\{Q_{T^{\prime}}, K_{I^{\prime}}, V_{I^{\prime}}\} = \{H_{T^{\prime}}W_{Q}, H_{I^{\prime}}W_{K}, H_{I^{\prime}}W_{V}\}$.



