\subsection{Main Results}
Following \cite{cai-etal-2019-multi}, we use Accuracy (Acc.), macro-average precision (P), macro-average recall (R), and macro-average F1 (F1) to report the performance of our model in Table \ref{tab:results_mmsd2}. Our main observations are follows: (1) \textbf{Our method MiDRE outperforms all SOTA baselines in MMSD2.0}: On MMSD2.0 dataset, MiDRE outperforms all baseline across unimodal and multimodal settings. Specifically it ourpeforms the best SOTA multimodal baseline Multi-view CLIP by good margins across metrics (Acc: $\uparrow 1.04\%$, P: $\uparrow 6.44\%$, R: $\uparrow 0.79\%$, F1: $\uparrow 3.79\%$ ). This proves the efficacy of using structured rationales and having dual reasoning paths to identify sarcasm. 

(2) \textbf{MiDRE outperforms all multimodal baselines that use external knowledge:}
We notice that our method significantly outperform baselines like HFM \cite{cai-etal-2019-multi} (Acc: $\uparrow 15.61\%$ in MMSD2.0) which uses image attributes generated using ResNet, CMGCN \cite{Liang2022MultiModalSD} (Acc: $\uparrow 6.35\%$ in MMSD2.0) which utilizes object-attribute pairs from a detectron module and HKE \cite{liu-etal-2022-towards-multi-modal} (Acc: $\uparrow 9.68\%$ in MMSD2.0) which incorporates image caption, image attributes and adjective-noun pairs from image. This highlights the importance of leveraging structured, verbose and sarcasm-oriented knowledge.




\subsection{Results on Biased MMSD Dataset}
MMSD is a text-biased dataset, as demonstrated by \citet{qin-etal-2023-mmsd2}. This bias arises from the presence of strong textual cues, such as hashtags and emojis that predominantly occur in one class. Since text-only models do not process visual information, they can directly exploit these cues without interference from the image modality, often outperforming multimodal models on this dataset. For completeness, we evaluate MiDRE on MMSD. As shown in Table \ref{tab:results_mmsd}, the text-only LLM ChatGLM2-6B even outperforms existing multimodal models. Notably, traditional language models like RoBERTa perform on par with larger LLMs such as LLaMA2-7B and ChatGLM2-6B, further emphasizing the dataset’s inherent bias. Despite this, MiDRE achieves consistently better performance than all prior multimodal approaches by significant margins, demonstrating its robust reasoning ability. Moreover, the small performance gap between MiDRE and ChatGLM2-6B suggests that MiDRE effectively leverages strong textual signals while also integrating complementary visual and external knowledge.

\section{Comparison with LVLMs}
We compare the performance of state-of-the-art large vision-language models (LVLMs) under a zero-shot prompting setup for MMSD2.0, as reported in Figure \ref{fig:lvlm_comp}. Despite advanced zero-shot capabilities and contextual knowledge, these models perform poorly on our task. Since fine-tuning of such large models is expensive, we press upon the need of developing smaller models like MiDRE that are explicitly designed for nuanced reasoning tasks such as multimodal sarcasm detection. At the same time, it reveals how the capabilities of such smaller models can be significantly amplified by strategically leveraging contextual understanding from large vision-language models.



\section{Ablation}
To further prove the efficacy of our approach, we answer the following research questions:
(1) \textit{What is the impact of having only one expert network?} 
(2) \textit{What advantage does the gating network provide? } 
(3) \textit{Do we need rationales from image, text and multimodal views?} 
% (4) \textit{Is a bottleneck-style gating network more effective than a simple linear gating mechanism? }

\subsection{Single Expert Network}
To better understand the contributions of the internal (IR) and external (ER) reasoning experts, we perform an ablation study on MiDRE by selectively removing each component (Table \ref{tab:ablation}, rows 3 \& 4). Removing the ER module (\textit{w/o ER}) leads to a significant drop in accuracy (↓4.72\% on MMSD, ↓3.91\% on MMSD2.0), highlighting the importance of external contextual rationales that are not directly inferable from the input. Conversely, removing IR (\textit{w/o IR}) also reduces performance (↓2.75\% on MMSD, ↓2.12\% on MMSD2.0), showing that ER alone struggles to compensate for noisy or incomplete rationales.

Interestingly, from Figure \ref{fig:venn}, we can observe that \textit{w/o ER} correctly classifies 111 unique samples and \textit{w/o IR} classifies 154 different ones, suggesting that both experts offer complementary and independently valuable reasoning capabilities. Moreover, the larger performance drop without ER indicates its dominant role, but IR remains essential for grounding predictions in the raw image-text content, enabling more reliable sarcasm detection through their combined use.


