\subsection{Need of External Rationale}
External reasoning is crucial in sarcasm detection, particularly when textual and visual cues alone fail to establish contradiction. For instance, in Figure \ref{fig:analysis1}(a), the image shows Easter-themed products on a shelf, while the text questions when Halloween products will arrive, implying that Easter items are being sold too early. A model without external knowledge may struggle to detect sarcasm since the image-text itself does not explicitly indicate any contradiction. However, external reasoning clarifies that Easter eggs appearing in January is unusual, reinforcing the sarcasm. MiDRE successfully classifies this instance as sarcastic, with a high gate value for External Rationale (ER) module (0.876), while the baseline and MiDRE without ER fail. In Figure \ref{fig:analysis1}(b), the text references a famous sports trade (Mike Ditka’s excessive trade for Ricky Williams) to suggest that the Broncos should do something similarly extreme. The accompanying image with confused expressions enhances the sarcasm. Without knowledge of the Ditka trade, the sarcasm might be missed, as the text could be interpreted as a genuine suggestion. External reasoning bridges this gap by explaining the historical context, leading MiDRE to detect sarcasm correctly (ER gate value: 0.921). In the case of Figure \ref{fig:analysis1}(c), both the image and text lack strong sarcastic elements. The image depicts a casual mirror selfie, and the text states that the person probably takes selfies with filters. MiDRE assigns a high ER gate value (0.781), suggesting that the Internal Reasoning (IR) module might be uncertain due to the overtly positive tone of the text. However, ER helps by recognizing that there is no cultural or contextual contradiction that would indicate sarcasm.

\subsection{Impact of Noisy Rationale}
External rationales, even when noisy or indirect, enhance the internal reasoning (IR) module by providing valuable contextual cues for sarcasm detection. For example, in Figure \ref{fig:analysis2}(a), MiDRE without external reasoning (ER) fails to detect sarcasm, while MiDRE succeeds by leveraging the image rationale, which highlights the neutral design of the seat. This neutrality amplifies the exaggerated praise in the text, making the sarcasm more apparent. Similarly, in \ref{fig:analysis2}(b), although the image rationale doesn’t directly contradict the caption, it describes the dog’s playful behavior, subtly clashing with the claim of it being "well-behaved" at the vet. In both cases, ER offers factual descriptions that help IR identify subtle incongruities, demonstrating the complementary role of external rationales in improving sarcastic intent recognition.

% \subsection{Impact of Noisy Rationale}
% External rationales provide contextual information, even if noisy or uncertain, still helps the internal reasoning (IR) module to make better sarcasm predictions. 
% For instance, in Figure \ref{fig:analysis2}(a), MiDRE without ER fails, but MiDRE correctly classifies the sarcasm, highlighting the importance of external rationales. The image rationale describes the seat as a standard design with no inherent sarcasm, which, on its own, does not signal contradiction. However, the image rationale provides additional context by confirming the neutral nature of the image, making the exaggerated praise in the text (“just awesome and elegant!”) stand out as sarcastic. This is reflected in the gate values. While the image-text contrast was not strong enough for IR alone to detect sarcasm, the contextual cues from ER helped MiDRE recognize the mismatch between the neutral image and the overly enthusiastic text. Similarly, in \ref{fig:analysis2}(b), the image rationale describes the dog's open mouth and tongue as signs of excitement or playfulness but does not explicitly contradict the claim that the dog is well-behaved. However, this description is still valuable because it highlights the dog's active demeanor, which contradicts the caption "well-behaved dog in a vet’s office", where calmness is usually expected. This contextual clue helps MiDRE infer the underlying sarcasm in the text, even though the ER does not state a direct contradiction. In both cases, the ER does not provide a definitive label for sarcasm but instead offers factual descriptions that serve as anchors for IR to interpret subtle mismatches.

